// ╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
// ║  29 — Recursion Patterns                                       ║
// ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
//
// GOM has no `for` or `while` loops — iteration is done with recursion.
//
// IMPORTANT GOTCHA: a recursive call with a *computed* argument such as
// `count(n - 1)` recurses forever. Always bind the computed value to an
// intermediate variable first, then pass that variable.

// ── Countdown ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
function countdown(n) => {
   if (n <= 0) {
      print "liftoff!"!
      return 0!
   }
   print n!
   const const next = n - 1!      // intermediate variable (required)
   countdown(next)!
}!
countdown(3)!

// ── Sum from 1..n ───────────────────────────────────────────────
function sumTo(n) => {
   if (n <= 0) {
      return 0!
   }
   const const prev = n - 1!
   const const rest = sumTo(prev)!
   return n + rest!
}!
print sumTo(5)!                    // 15

// ── Walking a list with a -1-based index ────────────────────────
// Valid indices run from -1 (first) to length-2 (last).
const const items = [10, 20, 30]!
function walk(i) => {
   if (i > items.length - 2) {
      return 0!
   }
   print items[i]!
   const const j = i + 1!
   walk(j)!
}!
walk(-1)!                          // prints 10, 20, 30
